## PRE-PROCESSING: SCIENTIFIC OBSERVATION PIPELINE (MANDATORY — run before any output)

Before writing any alt text, silently run these steps in order:

STEP 1 — FIGURE TYPE & GLOBAL PURPOSE
- State the figure type in one concise phrase (graph, radiograph, microscopy, tracing, diagram, chart, network, heatmap, flowchart, clinical photo, waveform).
- State what the figure is *visually comparing* or *demonstrating* (derived strictly from visible titles/labels/legend). Do NOT state interpretation or clinical diagnosis.

STEP 2 — LAYOUT MAP
- Count panels and subpanels (explicitly). Verify count twice.
- State arrangement (e.g., "four panels arranged two-by-two", "single panel with two insets: top-right and bottom-left").
- State presence of legends, insets, scale bars, and any overlaying elements.

STEP 3 — VARIABLE EXTRACTION
- List horizontal axis label and units, vertical axis label and units, legend labels and marker shapes, any printed numeric ticks, and any visible p values or statistical markers exactly as they appear.
- If waveform or ECG, list trace names printed (e.g., "His trace", "RA", "RVa") and visible sample rate/timebase if printed.

STEP 4 — OBJECT INVENTORY (per panel in reading order)
- For each panel: name visible objects generically (photo, scan, micrograph, bar graph, line trace), then enumerate visible textual labels inside the panel (exactly), callouts, arrows (tag arrow type if visually distinct), markers, and scale bars.
- For histology: note magnification and scale bar numeric label (if visible), tissue layers, cell densities, distribution patterns.
- For chemistry: list molecules shown, number of rings, annotated bond types, labels for substituents (if visible).
- For ECG/waveform: list deflections, H and H prime markers, PR intervals *using horizontal axis units displayed*, and any split His recording peaks visually labeled.

STEP 5 — SPATIAL RELATIONS
- Describe relative positions (object A is superior to B, or "adjacent to," "surrounding," "central", "at coordinate printed x,y") using panel coordinate words (topmost, bottommost, upper-left, second from left).
- For multi-panel: explicitly state reading order you use (default left-to-right top-to-bottom) and follow it.

STEP 6 — BEHAVIORAL / QUANTITATIVE DESCRIPTION (trends & numeric items)
- For graphs: describe full trend across the horizontal axis, list turning points, peaks, valleys, and relative ordering across series. For multiple series, explicitly compare them series-by-series.
- For heatmaps/omics: describe clustering (which rows/columns cluster together), axes labels, and value ranges if printed.
- For statistical figures: if significance letters/asterisks are visible, describe exactly which groups have which labels and the axis values they align with.
- For waveforms: enumerate deflections, timing relationships (e.g., PR prolongation before non-conduction), splitting of His (H and H prime), and widening of split if visible. Use precise time units if printed.

STEP 7 — OPTIONAL VISUAL OUTCOME (only when directly visible)
- Only state an outcome if the image *explicitly* contains wording such as "significant reduction" with p value or shows numeric comparative results. Do not infer.

STEP 8 — SINGLE-PARAGRAPH LD
- Long description must be a single paragraph that follows Object → Location → Relationship → Change → Outcome order and uses the reading order laid out in STEP 5.

